.TH SET_ITERATOR_VALUE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_iterator_value \- Get the value of the current element in a set using an iterator

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB char*
.IB set_iterator_value(const set_iterator_t* it, size_t* size_ptr);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_iterator_value\fR function gets the value of the current element in a set using an iterator. It takes two arguments: the iterator and a pointer to a size_t to store the size of the value. The function returns a pointer to the value if the iterator is valid, and NULL if the iterator is invalid.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB it
The iterator to use to get the value of the current element.
.TP
.IB size_ptr
A pointer to a size_t to store the size of the value.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns a pointer to the value of the current element if the iterator is valid, and NULL if the iterator is invalid.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_iterator_value\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    set_iterator_t it;
    set_init(&set);

    set_inserts(&set, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set, "cherry");

    if(set_iterator_init(&it, &set)) {
        printf("Iterator initialized successfully\n");
        while(set_iterator_next(&it)) {
            char* value;
            size_t size;
            value = set_iterator_value(&it, &size);
            if(value) {
                printf("Element: %.*s\n", (int)size, value);
            } else {
                printf("No more elements\n");
            }
        }
    } else {
        printf("Failed to initialize iterator\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef